From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 06:28:51 -0400
Subject: feat: Stedding updates itself on the Mac, and says thank you after an
 install and an update

The Mac gains an updater (docs/features/updates.md, ADR 0022). Sparkle 2, which
tooling/fetch-sparkle puts at third_party/stedding_sparkle, is bundled into
Contents/Frameworks when stedding_sparkle is set, and the browser loads it at
runtime and is both its user driver and its delegate, so no Sparkle window opens.
It checks a signed feed (SUFeedURL, SUPublicEDKey, SURequireSignedFeed and
SUVerifyUpdateBeforeExtraction in app-Info.plist) once a day and downloads in the
background; a downloaded version waits behind "New Stedding Version Available" at
the sidebar's foot, above the Space row, as Arc does, and a click restarts into it
with the session restored. The About page checks when it opens, shows the
download, and its Relaunch restarts through the updater; the command bar lists
Restart to Update Stedding and Check for Updates, and the Stedding menu has Check
for Updates... under About. "Update Stedding automatically" in Settings > Stedding >
Updates turns it off, and then Sparkle is never started. Only a bundle signed by a
team starts it, so a local build never replaces itself.

CFBundleVersion now carries Stedding's version (10020.16 for 0.2.0-beta.16;
tweak_info_plist.py), which Sparkle and LaunchServices compare, and Chromium's
signer signs Sparkle's helpers and framework before the app (parts.py).

After the install, when the welcome flow ends, and on the first launch of each
later version, the browser opens one thank-you page on stedding.dev; the
update's names the version in its fragment, and "Show a thank-you page after an
update" turns it off. The version that ran is kept in local state.

Why: the owner asked for automatic updates like Arc's and for the thank-you pages
(2026-10-16), and a Chromium fork that cannot deliver its own security updates
leaves its users behind.
Removable when: never as a whole; the updater is Stedding's own, and a move to
another mechanism (Chromium's updater, a Windows one) replaces the Sparkle half.
